// A closure with no captures (Ch. 4 §4.1).
//
// It is three things at once and the shape is worth reading whole: a
// **type**, whose fields are the addresses of what it captured — none here,
// so a type with no fields, which is still a tryte because storage always
// has an address; a **function**, `<name>.call`, taking that type by
// reference and then what was written; and a **type argument**, because
// `impl Fn(A) -> R` in argument position is a type parameter nobody wrote
// (§2.2), so `apply` is one function per closure passed to it.
//
// The name is `main.closure1` — which closure of which function, and
// nothing about how either was numbered (G9.67).
//
// A closure that **captures** is here too. `k` inside the body is a field
// of the closure holding `k`'s address, so it is read as `(*self.k)` — and
// every rule about fields and references then applies to it unchanged. That
// needed the one expression this lowering had never had: a dereference of a
// reference *value*, which needs the emitter to carry `&t27` and not just
// `ptr`, because TIR spells every reference the same way and only the name
// says how wide the thing at the other end is.
//
// The `.call` functions come out most-recently-found first, which is what a
// stack gives back — the same question G9.58 asked about instantiations.

fn apply(g: impl Fn(t27) -> t27, x: t27) -> t27 { g(x) }

fn none(g: impl Fn() -> t27) -> t27 { g() }

fn twice(g: impl Fn(t27) -> t27, x: t27) -> t27 { apply(g, x) }

fn main() -> t27 {
    let k = 10;
    // Bound by a `let`, which renames the temporary it was built in.
    let f = |x: t27| x + 1;
    // A block body, two parameters, a written answer, and a capture.
    let h = |a: t27, b: t27| -> t27 { a * b + k };
    // Written where it is used, and one that captures nothing at all.
    apply(f, 2) + apply(|y: t27| y * k, 3) + none(|| 7) + h(2, 3)
        + twice(|z: t27| z - k, 4)
}
